<Docs
  title="Tools"
  description="Developer tools as Durable Object apps: an ESM CDN, user code as ESM, a bundler, an Astro builder and a notebook compiler, on Wrangler, celld and CellB."
>
  <h1>Tools</h1>
  <p>
    The tools that turn sources into running apps are Durable Object apps themselves:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>a package store that caches the npm registry for the other tools;</li>
    <li>a CDN that serves npm packages as ES modules;</li>
    <li>a server that serves a project's own files as ES modules;</li>
    <li>a bundler that turns a project into one Worker;</li>
    <li>an Astro builder, and a notebook compiler.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    They are composed into one Worker, <a href={docHref("packages/platform/README.md")}>the
    platform</a>. Like any project on CellB, it runs unchanged on Wrangler (workerd, as on
    Cloudflare), on celld, on CellB in Node, and on CellB in a browser tab. What a tool builds runs
    on the same hosts.
  </p>

  <h2>Use cases</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>You want to</th><th>Use</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Import npm packages in a page or a Worker with no install step and no third-party CDN at run time</td>
        <td>The ESM CDN: <code>/npm/browser/react</code> redirects to a pinned, immutable module; <code>?graph</code> lists every module to preload</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Run a TypeScript/TSX project in a browser as it is written: an editor, a playground, an AI agent writing code</td>
        <td>User code as ESM: write files, take a snapshot, open <code>/p/&lt;id&gt;/s/&lt;snapshot&gt;/~/index.html</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Turn a project into one package: a download, a <code>no_bundle</code> wrangler project, a Worker Loader payload</td>
        <td>The bundler: <code>POST /builds</code> with a ZIP, then <code>/b/&lt;id&gt;/project.zip</code> or <code>/b/&lt;id&gt;/loader.json</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Build an Astro site (EmDash included) without Node or Vite: in a Durable Object, or in the visitor's tab</td>
        <td>The Astro builder, through the same <code>POST /builds</code>: a project with an <code>astro.config.*</code> is built from its sources</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Publish Markdown or Observable Notebook Kit notebooks as live pages</td>
        <td>Notebooks: <code>POST /nb/&lt;site&gt;/build</code>, then open <code>/nb/&lt;site&gt;/&lt;page&gt;.html</code></td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>The HTTP API</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Route</th><th>App (Durable Object)</th><th>What it answers</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>GET /registry/&lt;name&gt;</code>, <code>…/-/&lt;file&gt;.tgz</code></td><td>package store (<code>PackageRegistry</code>, one per package)</td><td>the npm registry, cached: packuments for 10 minutes, tarballs forever</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>GET /npm/&lt;browser|node&gt;/&lt;name&gt;[@range][/sub]</code></td><td>ESM CDN (<code>ModuleHost</code>, one per profile)</td><td>302 to the pinned URL; the pinned URL is the module as ESM; <code>?graph</code>, <code>?meta</code>, <code>?raw</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>PUT /p/&lt;id&gt;/files/&lt;path&gt;</code>, <code>POST /p/&lt;id&gt;/import</code></td><td>user code (<code>Project</code>, one per project)</td><td>writes the working tree (a file, a ZIP or JSON)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>POST /p/&lt;id&gt;/snapshots</code>, <code>GET /p/&lt;id&gt;/s/&lt;snap&gt;/~/&lt;path&gt;</code></td><td>user code</td><td>freezes the tree under its hash; serves a source as ESM (TS, TSX, JSX, CommonJS, CSS as <code>?module</code>)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>POST /builds</code> (ZIP or JSON, <code>?project=…&amp;snapshot=…</code>)</td><td>bundler (<code>BuildJob</code>, one per build)</td><td>builds in the object or queues for the Node runner; the build id is the hash of the builder version, the inputs and the spec</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>GET /b/&lt;id&gt;/project.zip</code>, <code>/b/&lt;id&gt;/loader.json</code>, <code>/b/&lt;id&gt;/&lt;file&gt;</code></td><td>bundler</td><td>the output: a wrangler project, a Worker Loader payload, any file</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>POST /nb/&lt;site&gt;/import</code>, <code>/build</code>; <code>GET /nb/&lt;site&gt;/…</code></td><td>notebooks (<code>NotebookSite</code>, one per site)</td><td>compiles notebooks to pages; serves the pages and their modules</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="sh"
    code={`curl -L localhost:8787/npm/browser/react                       # the ESM CDN
curl 'localhost:8787/npm/browser/react-dom@19/client?graph'   # the modules to preload

(cd my-app && zip -r - .) | curl -X POST --data-binary @- localhost:8787/p/my-app/import
curl -X POST localhost:8787/p/my-app/snapshots                # → { base }: open <base>~/index.html

(cd my-worker && zip -r - .) | curl -X POST --data-binary @- localhost:8787/builds
curl -o build.zip localhost:8787/b/<id>/project.zip           # wrangler dev, celld dev, or CellB`}
  />

  <h2>How they work</h2>

  <h3>Every tool is a set of Durable Objects</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Each object keeps its files in its own SQLite (<code>ctx.storage.sql</code>), through a FilesApi
      that stores blocks of at most 256 KiB. That is under Cloudflare's 2 MB value limit, and celld
      and CellB store cells in the same format.
    </li>
    <li>
      Objects are named by what they hold: one per npm package in the package store, one per
      project, one per build, one per notebook site.
    </li>
    <li>
      The tools reach each other through stubs within one script, because celld and CellB support
      no service bindings to other scripts.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Packages come from the package store</h3>
  <p>
    Every tool loads npm packages through webrun-modules' <code>npmRegistrySource</code>, with a
    <code>fetch</code> that sends registry requests to the package store. A package is downloaded
    once per deployment, and the Node runner reads it over HTTP. Peer dependencies take the
    project's version, as npm installs them.
  </p>

  <h3>Module by module, or one bundle</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The ESM CDN and user code</strong> transform one file at a time with
      <code>@statewalker/webrun-modules</code>:
      <ul>
        <li>TypeScript and JSX are stripped and compiled;</li>
        <li>CommonJS is wrapped;</li>
        <li>CSS goes through Lightning CSS;</li>
        <li>every bare import becomes a same-origin URL.</li>
      </ul>
      Pinned versions and snapshot ids are in the URLs, so every response is immutable.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The bundler</strong> runs <code>@statewalker/webrun-bundler</code> on esbuild-wasm:
      <ul>
        <li>one Worker module, with <code>cloudflare:*</code> and <code>node:*</code> left to the
          runtime;</li>
        <li>assets and D1 migrations copied;</li>
        <li>the fixes one build needs to run on every host (KV namespace ids,
          <code>node:module</code>'s <code>createRequire</code>, celld's internal tables).</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Builds are placed</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>A small build runs in its <code>BuildJob</code> object.</li>
    <li>
      A build larger than <code>IN_OBJECT_BUILD_MAX_BYTES</code> is queued. A Node runner
      (<code>packages/bundler/bin/webrun-build.ts --runner &lt;origin&gt;</code>) claims it, builds it with the same
      code and native esbuild, and uploads the output.
    </li>
    <li>
      Cloudflare's isolates have 128 MB, and EmDash's build needs far more. On hosts without that
      limit, EmDash builds inside the object too: about 35 s on the Node runner, against 87 to
      146 s inside the object (87 s on CellB in Node, 102 s on celld, 146 s on Wrangler).
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Astro without Vite, wherever esbuild runs</h3>
  <p>
    The Astro builder is the in-browser builder of
    <a href={docHref("examples/astro-in-browser/README.md")}>Astro built in a tab</a>, made a
    package:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>astro.config.mjs</code> and its integrations run;</li>
    <li>routes come from <code>src/pages</code> and from the integrations;</li>
    <li><code>.astro</code> files are compiled by compiler-rs as WebAssembly;</li>
    <li>webrun-bundler builds the server and client bundles.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The project's config is code, and workerd and celld refuse to evaluate code at run time. There,
    the whole build runs in a <a href={docHref("packages/cellb/docs/services/dynamic-workers.md")}>Worker
    Loader</a> isolate: the builder, the bundled config and the WebAssembly are its modules, and the
    project's build-time code is isolated from the platform. CellB and browsers evaluate the config
    directly. A browser tab must be cross-origin isolated, for the compiler's shared memory.
  </p>

  <h3>One build per host</h3>
  <p>The platform's <code>scripts/build.mjs</code> writes two folders:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      one for workerd and celld, with the WebAssembly as module imports, since those runtimes refuse
      to compile it from bytes;
    </li>
    <li>one for CellB, with the WebAssembly embedded.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <code>build:browser</code> turns the CellB folder into a static site that runs every tool in a
    tab: a host page, a ServiceWorker and a shard worker with SQLite on OPFS.
  </p>

  <h2>What is checked</h2>
  <p>
    The end-to-end scripts start the platform on each host and check every tool on small and large
    projects. They then run what was built on the same host and check its pages, its Durable
    Objects and its hydration in Chromium.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Check</th><th>Wrangler</th><th>celld</th><th>CellB, Node</th><th>CellB, Chromium tab</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>ESM CDN: react-dom, Observable Plot with d3</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td></tr>
      <tr><td>User code as ESM: a React page renders</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Bundler: a Hono app with a Durable Object, built and run</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td><td>built in the tab</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Astro app built from its sources, run, island hydrated</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td><td>built in the tab</td></tr>
      <tr><td>EmDash built from its sources (225 routes): setup seeds D1, pages render</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td><td>not tried</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Notebooks compiled in the object, cells run</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The demo apps are in <a href={docHref("examples/platform-demos/README.md")}>Apps built by the
    tools</a>. Each package's page has its API: the
    <a href={docHref("packages/esm-cdn/README.md")}>ESM CDN</a>, the
    <a href={docHref("packages/bundler/README.md")}>bundler</a>, the
    <a href={docHref("packages/astro-builder/README.md")}>Astro builder</a>,
    <a href={docHref("packages/notebooks/README.md")}>notebooks</a>, and the
    <a href={docHref("packages/platform-core/README.md")}>package store</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Run it</h2>
  <CodeBlock
    lang="sh"
    code={`pnpm install                                   # at the repository root
cd packages/platform
pnpm run build                                 # dist/platform/workerd and dist/platform/cellb
npx wrangler dev                               # on workerd, from the sources
npx tsx scripts/serve-cellb.mjs dist/platform/cellb --port 8793   # on CellB in Node
pnpm run build:browser && python3 -m http.server -d dist/browser 8080   # in a tab: open /__cellb/
npx tsx scripts/e2e.ts all                     # every check on Wrangler, celld and CellB in Node
pnpm run e2e:browser                           # the platform in a Chromium tab`}
  />
</Docs>
